    "q-171896": {
      "dom_id": "step-q171896",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191212/assets/bbc303a7d1ee4bcad3bdbd29b4897e972556bfe89bdff2831aaeef642026b98b.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the measure of $∠D$ is equal to{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle. Consequently, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠ABC={72}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $△CDB$ is an isosceles triangle, we have:\n\n$$\nm∠D=m∠BCD\n$$\n\nSince $∠ABC$ and $∠CBD$ are supplementary, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ABC+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {72}^{∘}+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠CBD & ={108}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $m∠D$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠D+m∠BCD+m∠CBD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D+m∠D+{108}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠D & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={36}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
